From aef51555e016703df3d25a36a56a9b3a691f8600 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vladyslav Horbachov <0965142838gvs@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 11:33:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] refactor(sdk): one browser login rail for Gmail and
 Salesforce

Sdk/OAuth replaces the two hand-rolled login pipelines with one:

- BrowserLogins is the per-provider one-use request registry: mint for the
  current verified turn, open once, claim once on the callback, publish
  credentials only inside commitIfActive (publication and cancellation share
  a lock), resume the durable turn exactly once. The Gmail semantics are the
  base: reference-free turn matching, cancellation registration, the
  "already resumed" guard, and the IsWaiting check before completion, which
  closes the Salesforce race where the callback could finish before the
  turn published its login card.
- BrowserLoginSurface maps the login and callback paths with the private
  headers, trace redaction, GET/origin guard and one-use begin; it is an
  IHttpSurface, so the kernel keeps its single UseModuleHttpSurfaces call.
- BrowserLoginCorrelation is the one-use claim both OAuth handlers add after
  the framework's correlation check; LoginPage renders the fixed-string
  result pages; BrowserLoginWorker<TLogins> is the completion worker.

Gmail keeps GmailLogins (definition, read tools, compose scope), the OIDC
scheme and Google identity checks, GmailConnections and the draft previews;
Salesforce keeps SalesforceLogins, the OAuth scheme, and a SalesforceConnections
that is now only the credential store. Both completion workers, both
middlewares and the Salesforce pending-login state machine are gone.

BrowserLoginsTests cover the registry's state machine, which previously had
no test at all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E4GTMuXrb6yjgUfhPpzgD7
